m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: The network uses the strongest prebiotic entries into alanine: (i) Strecker conversion of acetaldehyde to alanine nitrile followed by hydrolysis, (ii) Bucherer-Bergs-type trapping of the same C2 aldehyde as 5-methylhydantoin followed by hydrolysis, and (iii) reductive amination of pyruvate. Formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde provide an upstream C1/C2 surface branch to acetaldehyde, while lactonitrile provides a cyanohydrin branch to pyruvate. Because robust prebiotic syntheses are expected to be racemic, the network ends with a separate mineral-interface enantioenrichment step to reach L-alanine. The least secure nodes are glycolaldehyde deoxygenation to acetaldehyde and the final chiral selection step.\n\nMOLECULES (14 nodes):\n  - mol_001: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_004: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_005: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_006: C2H4O2 (glycolaldehyde) [intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C2H4O (acetaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C3H6N2 (alanine nitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C3H8N2O (alaninamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C4H6N2O2 (5-methylhydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C3H5NO (lactonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C3H4O3 (pyruvic acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C3H7NO2 (DL-alanine) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C3H7NO2 (L-alanine) [target]\n\nREACTIONS (12 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_005 -> mol_006 (Formaldehyde dimerization to glycolaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_006 -> mol_007 (Glycolaldehyde deoxygenation to acetaldehyde) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_003: mol_007, mol_002, mol_001 -> mol_008 (Strecker formation of alanine nitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_008, mol_003 -> mol_013 (Direct hydrolysis of alanine nitrile to DL-alanine) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_005: mol_008, mol_003 -> mol_009 (Partial hydrolysis of alanine nitrile to alaninamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_009, mol_003 -> mol_013 (Hydrolysis of alaninamide to DL-alanine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_007, mol_002, mol_001, mol_004 -> mol_010 (Bucherer-Bergs-type formation of 5-methylhydantoin) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_010, mol_003 -> mol_013 (Hydantoin hydrolysis to DL-alanine) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_009: mol_007, mol_001 -> mol_011 (Cyanohydrin formation from acetaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_011, mol_003 -> mol_012 (Lactonitrile hydrolysis-oxidation to pyruvate) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_012, mol_002 -> mol_013 (Pyruvate reductive amination to DL-alanine) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_012: mol_013 -> mol_014 (Mineral-mediated L-enrichment of alanine) [Biochemical]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Direct Strecker-hydrolysis from acetaldehyde (3 steps)\n  - P2: Stepwise amide hydrolysis from acetaldehyde (4 steps)\n  - P3: Hydantoin route from acetaldehyde (3 steps)\n  - P4: Cyanohydrin to pyruvate from acetaldehyde (4 steps)\n  - P5: Direct pyruvate entry (2 steps)\n  - P6: Formaldehyde-derived acetaldehyde to direct Strecker (5 steps)\n  - P7: Formaldehyde-derived acetaldehyde to stepwise amide hydrolysis (6 steps)\n  - P8: Formaldehyde-derived acetaldehyde to hydantoin (5 steps)\n  - P9: Formaldehyde-derived acetaldehyde to pyruvate (6 steps)\n  - P10: Glycolaldehyde-derived acetaldehyde to direct Strecker (4 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 12 reactions | 3 balanced as written | 2 balanceable with coefficients | 7 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_001 balanceable: 2 CH2O -> C2H4O2\n  - rxn_002 UNBALANCED: C2H4O2 -> C2H4O | reactants minus products: O+1 | balanceable if adding: O2 as product\n  - rxn_003 UNBALANCED: C2H4O + NH3 + HCN -> C3H6N2 | reactants minus products: H+2, O+1 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product; CO2 as product; O2 as product\n  - rxn_004 UNBALANCED: C3H6N2 + H2O -> C3H7NO2 | reactants minus products: H+1, N+1, O-1 | balanceable if adding: NH3 as product\n  - rxn_006 UNBALANCED: C3H8N2O + H2O -> C3H7NO2 | reactants minus products: H+3, N+1 | balanceable if adding: NH3 as product\n  - rxn_007 balanceable: 6 C2H4O + 2 NH3 + 12 HCN + 4 CO2 -> 7 C4H6N2O2\n  - rxn_008 UNBALANCED: C4H6N2O2 + H2O -> C3H7NO2 | reactants minus products: C+1, H+1, N+1, O+1 | no single common small species (H2O, H2, NH3, CO2, ...) fixes it\n  - rxn_010 UNBALANCED: C3H5NO + H2O -> C3H4O3 | reactants minus products: H+3, N+1, O-1 | no single common small species (H2O, H2, NH3, CO2, ...) fixes it\n  - rxn_011 UNBALANCED: C3H4O3 + NH3 -> C3H7NO2 | reactants minus products: O+1 | balanceable if adding: O2 as product\nFormula/structure inconsistencies:\n  - mol_008: InChI could not be parsed by RDKit (RDKit could not parse InChI)\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so th