nfidence\": 0.41,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"Network is connected and has sufficient node count, and it spans several chemically recognizable entries into alanine. However, validity is limited by the RDKit ground truth: 7/12 reactions are UNBALANCED, and rxn_007 is only balanceable with implausibly large coefficients, indicating mis-specified stoichiometry. The most repairable issues are rxn_003 (should release H2O), rxn_004 (should release NH3 and needs 2 H2O overall), rxn_006 (should release NH3), rxn_007 (likely missing H2O), rxn_008 (needs NH3 and CO2 loss and/or an N-carbamoyl intermediate), and rxn_011 (needs an explicit reductant and H2O byproduct). The weakest chemistry is rxn_002, which gives no plausible oxygen sink or reductant for glycolaldehyde -> acetaldehyde, and rxn_010, which should not map lactonitrile directly to pyruvate without separate hydrolysis to lactate-type products and an oxidation step. rxn_012 is environmentally inconsistent as labeled ('Biochemical' vs mineral-mediated) and does not justify direct conversion of DL-alanine to pure L-alanine; at best it could represent a partial physical resolution. Broad alignment with prebiotic literature is strongest for the Strecker/cyanohydrin parts and weakest for rxn_002, rxn_010, and the chiral-selection step. Check mol_008 as well, because its InChI failed RDKit parsing. As written, the network robustly supports at most DL-alanine through some branches and is not approved for L-alanine.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_003": 0.72, "rxn_004": 0.6, "rxn_012": 0.25}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.56, "pathway_coherence": 0.78, "environmental_progression": 0.56, "target_achievement": 0.38, "overall_confidence": 0.57, "feedback": "Core Strecker chemistry from acetaldehyde is the strongest branch in the network. rxn_003 is chemically plausible but should expel H2O, and rxn_004 should release NH3 and use two waters overall. Surface-to-hydrothe