{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T08:30:04.237193", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: L-Alanine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.41\n- Feedback: Network is connected and has sufficient node count, and it spans several chemically recognizable entries into alanine. However, validity is limited by the RDKit ground truth: 7/12 reactions are UNBALANCED, and rxn_007 is only balanceable with implausibly large coefficients, indicating mis-specified stoichiometry. The most repairable issues are rxn_003 (should release H2O), rxn_004 (should release NH3 and needs 2 H2O overall), rxn_006 (should release NH3), rxn_007 (likely missing H2O), rxn_008 (needs NH3 and CO2 loss and/or an N-carbamoyl intermediate), and rxn_011 (needs an explicit reductant and H2O byproduct). The weakest chemistry is rxn_002, which gives no plausible oxygen sink or reductant for glycolaldehyde -> acetaldehyde, and rxn_010, which should not map lactonitrile directly to pyruvate without separate hydrolysis to lactate-type products and an oxidation step. rxn_012 is environmentally inconsistent as labeled ('Biochemical' vs mineral-mediated) and does not justify direct conversion of DL-alanine to pure L-alanine; at best it could represent a partial physical resolution. Broad alignment with prebiotic literature is strongest for the Strecker/cyanohydrin parts and weakest for rxn_002, rxn_010, and the chiral-selection step. Check mol_008 as well, because its InChI failed RDKit parsing. As written, the network robustly supports at most DL-alanine through some branches and is not approved for L-alanine.\n- Issues: ['Core Strecker chemistry from acetaldehyde is the strongest branch in the network. rxn_003 is chemically plausible but should expel H2O, and rxn_004 should release NH3 and use two waters overall. Surface-to-hydrothermal transfer is assumed rather than justified, and rxn_012 is better treated as mineral resolution than a biochemical reaction. This pathway supports DL-alanine much better than isolated L-alanine.', 'The aminonitrile -> amide -> amino acid sequence is coherent and rxn_005 is properly balanced, but rxn_003 still omits H2O and rxn_006 omits NH3. Keeping both hydrolyses on a generic surface is less convincing than heated aqueous processing. As with all branches, rxn_012 does not yet demonstrate production of pure L-alanine.', 'Hydantoin chemistry is a recognized amino acid entry, but this implementation is under-specified. rxn_007 only balances with implausible coefficients, suggesting a missing H2O product, and rxn_008 needs additional byproducts such as CO2 and NH3 or an explicit N-carbamoyl alanine intermediate. The final enantioenrichment step remains the limiting issue.', 'Cyanohydrin formation is strong, but the route breaks at pyruvate generation. rxn_010 should not convert lactonitrile directly to pyruvate by simple hydrolysis; a lactate-type intermediate and a separate oxidation step are needed. rxn_011 also lacks the reductant required for reductive amination. The branch does not convincingly reach L-alanine as written.', 'This is only a terminal segment rather than a full feedstock-to-target route, and it relies on an under-specified pyruvate chemistry. rxn_011 needs an explicit reducing system and proper stoichiometry, because NH3 alone will not reductively aminate pyruvate. Combined with the weak chiral-selection step, target achievement is low.', 'The formaldehyde -> glycolaldehyde entry is plausible, but rxn_002 is the weakest single reaction in the network: glycolaldehyde -> acetaldehyde is unbalanced and lacks a credible redox partner or oxygen sink. Downstream Strecker chemistry is reasonable after stoichiometric repair. The pathway still reaches at best DL-alanine robustly, not well-supported L-alanine.', 'This branch combines a plausible formose-type start with a weaker midstream deoxygenation. rxn_002 remains a major bottleneck, and rxn_003/rxn_006 both omit expected byproducts. The amide branch is coherent once alanine nitrile exists, but the overall path is still undermined by the unsupported acetaldehyde step and the unresolved final L-enrichment.', 'The hydantoin branch inherits the same weak glycolaldehyde -> acetaldehyde step and adds further stoichiometric problems in the hydantoin chemistry. rxn_007 appears to be missing H2O, and rxn_008 needs NH3/CO2 loss or an explicit carbamoyl intermediate. The path is chemically recognizable but not valid enough as written.', 'This is the least coherent full upstream route. It depends on the unconvincing deoxygenation rxn_002, the flawed lactonitrile -> pyruvate shortcut in rxn_010, the under-specified reductive amination rxn_011, and the weak final enantioenrichment step. Too many key nodes are chemically or stoichiometrically unsupported.', 'Starting from glycolaldehyde shortens the upstream branch, but the deoxygenation to acetaldehyde is still a major unresolved step. Once acetaldehyde is available, the Strecker/hydrolysis chemistry is reasonable after stoichiometric correction. The path again supports DL-alanine far better than a validated route to L-alanine.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Alanine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Use acetaldehyde directly as the C2 carbonyl feedstock rather than forcing a weak deoxygenation from glycolaldehyde. From acetaldehyde, HCN, and NH3, the network builds alanine chemistry by two Strecker modes: a dry-film imine route and a one-pot aqueous Strecker route. A parallel cyanohydrin branch gives lactate, then pyruvate, then alanine by explicit H2-dependent reductive amination on metal-rich geothermal surfaces. Aminonitriles are converted to an alanine pool either directly or stepwise via alaninamide. Because robust prebiotic evidence for fully homochiral alanine synthesis is weak, the target node represents the isolated L-enriched fraction obtained from that alanine pool by mineral-interface selection or eutectic crystallization in prebiotic pools. All key byproducts and redox partners are explicit, and no unsupported oxygen-sink or reductant-free shortcut is used.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanimine\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alanine nitrile (racemic/unspecified pool)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alaninamide (racemic/unspecified pool)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alanine (racemic/unspecified pool)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactonitrile (racemic/unspecified pool)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactic acid (racemic/unspecified pool)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine (isolated enriched fraction)\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible acetaldehyde imine formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"strong wet-dry cycling in NH3-bearing evaporitic or geothermal films; low water activity favors imine accumulation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to acetaldehyde to form a carbinolamine, which dehydrates during concentration to give ethanimine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aldehyde-ammonia condensation is a standard pre-Strecker step. Resolving ethanimine explicitly gives a chemically justified branch of alanine nitrile formation under drying conditions and avoids unsupported deoxygenation chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker cyanide addition to ethanimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyanide-bearing shallow pools or concentrated films; mildly basic NH3/HCN mixtures\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the C=N center of ethanimine to form the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanide addition to an imine or iminium is the canonical C-C bond-forming step of Strecker chemistry, making alanine nitrile a direct product from acetaldehyde-derived ethanimine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN-bearing surface waters or aerosols; reversible cyanohydrin equilibrium\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon of acetaldehyde and protonation gives lactonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin formation from aldehydes and HCN is fast and reversible in cyanide-bearing aqueous settings, so lactonitrile is a plausible alternative C3 reservoir derived from the same feedstocks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Overall Strecker synthesis of alanine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"one-pot aqueous NH3/HCN pool; transient imine formation but no isolated imine required\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net Strecker sequence in a single pool: transient imine formation followed by cyanide capture to the aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"One-pot Strecker synthesis can generate alanine nitrile directly from acetaldehyde, NH3, and HCN even when free ethanimine does not build up to high concentration. Including this net route captures a second experimentally plausible regime distinct from the dry-film imine branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct hydrolysis of alanine nitrile to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"prolonged heating in volcanic hot-spring or subaerial geothermal waters; acidic or mildly basic microenvironments accelerate hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Successive hydration of the nitrile followed by cleavage yields the carboxylic acid and releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Heated aqueous hydrolysis of alpha-aminonitriles gives amino acids with NH3 release. The product is represented as an alanine pool without fixed stereochemistry because achiral synthesis and hot aqueous processing do not reliably preserve a single enantiomer.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Partial hydrolysis of alanine nitrile to alaninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"geothermal aqueous films or pools with moderate heating\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"First hydration of the nitrile furnishes the corresponding amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydrolysis is mechanistically stepwise, and an amide is the chemically expected intermediate. Including alaninamide keeps oxygen accounting explicit and avoids the imbalances common in oversimplified prebiotic schemes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of alaninamide to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"continued heating in the same geothermal waters that support amide formation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amide hydrolysis converts the carboxamide to the carboxylic acid and releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Alaninamide is a balanceable and chemically standard intermediate on the route to alanine. Writing NH3 explicitly corrects a common omission in abbreviated nitrile-to-amino-acid schemes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of lactonitrile to lactic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"hot-spring or subaerial geothermal waters with prolonged contact times\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydration and cleavage of the nitrile group convert the cyanohydrin nitrile into the corresponding alpha-hydroxy acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin nitriles hydrolyze to alpha-hydroxy acids under heated aqueous conditions. This provides a chemically distinct branch from the same HCN-acetaldehyde pool without any pyruvate shortcut.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Mineral-mediated oxidation of lactic acid to pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni-Fe sulfide surfaces\", \"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm to mildly elevated in porous hot-spring conduits\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni-Fe sulfide surfaces / magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"subaerial geothermal field with metal-rich crusts; H2 escape helps pull dehydrogenation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"An alpha-hydroxy acid is dehydrogenated on redox-active mineral surfaces to the corresponding alpha-keto acid, with H2 as the explicit reduced product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lactate/pyruvate is a plausible mineral-mediated redox couple under anoxic geothermal conditions. This is less experimentally secure than Strecker chemistry, but it is chemically defensible when H2 is written explicitly as the electron sink, avoiding any hidden oxidant.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of pyruvic acid to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni-Fe sulfide surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm to mildly elevated in porous hot-spring conduits\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni-Fe sulfide surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"NH3-rich reducing geothermal pores with dissolved or freshly generated H2\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate first forms an imine or iminium with ammonia, then H2-driven hydrogenation on metal sulfides gives alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pyruvate can be converted to alanine only if both the nitrogen source and the reductant are explicit. Including H2 and H2O makes the redox chemistry transparent and repairs the reductant-free pyruvate-to-alanine shortcut that often fails stoichiometrically.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Chiral mineral-interface enrichment of L-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"chiral calcite or alpha-quartz step edges\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"chiral mineral step edges\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"adsorption-desorption cycling at mineral interfaces in prebiotic pools\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Diastereomeric adsorption at chiral mineral step edges partitions alanine enantiomers, allowing recovery of an L-enriched fraction after repeated cycles.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a physical resolution step, not a claim that a racemic pool quantitatively converts into one enantiomer. The target node denotes the isolated L-enriched fraction accumulated at or recovered from the mineral interface after repeated partitioning cycles.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Eutectic crystallization enrichment of L-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"evaporite salts\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"repeated evaporation-dissolution cycling in saline prebiotic pools; eutectic or fractional crystallization regime\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Differential crystal growth, solubility, and phase partitioning during wet-dry cycles enrich the harvestable L-alanine fraction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Repeated crystallization can preserve and amplify slight stochastic or surface-induced enantiomeric imbalances in amino-acid pools. As in rxn_011, the target node represents the recovered L-enriched fraction rather than total conversion of the whole alanine pool.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot Strecker direct hydrolysis with mineral selection\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde, NH3, and HCN form alanine nitrile in one aqueous Strecker step; hot aqueous hydrolysis gives alanine, and chiral mineral interfaces isolate an L-enriched fraction.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot Strecker direct hydrolysis with eutectic selection\",\n      \"description\": \"The same one-pot Strecker branch is followed by direct aminonitrile hydrolysis and then eutectic crystallization to recover L-enriched alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot Strecker amide branch with mineral selection\",\n      \"description\": \"Alanine nitrile from the overall Strecker route first hydrolyzes to alaninamide, then to alanine, before chiral mineral partitioning yields L-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"