tion to give ethanimine.", "reasoning": "Aldehyde-ammonia condensation is a standard pre-Strecker step. Resolving ethanimine explicitly gives a chemically justified branch of alanine nitrile formation under drying conditions and avoids unsupported deoxygenation chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Strecker cyanide addition to ethanimine", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "cyanide-bearing shallow pools or concentrated films; mildly basic NH3/HCN mixtures"}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the C=N center of ethanimine to form the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Cyanide addition to an imine or iminium is the canonical C-C bond-forming step of Strecker chemistry, making alanine nitrile a direct product from acetaldehyde-derived ethanimine."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "280-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN-bearing surface waters or aerosols; reversible cyanohydrin equilibrium"}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon of acetaldehyde and protonation gives lactonitrile.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin formation from aldehydes and HCN is fast and reversible in cyanide-bearing aqueous settings, so lactonitrile is a plausible alternative C3 reservoir derived from the same feedstocks."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Overall Strecker synthesis of alanine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "290-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "one-pot aqueous NH3/HCN pool; transient imine formation but no isolated imine required"}, "mechanism": "Net Strecker sequence in a single pool: transient imine formation followed by cyanide capture to the aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "One-pot Strecker synthesis can generate alanine nitrile directly from acetaldehyde, NH3, and HCN even when free ethanimine does not build up to high concentration. Including this net route captures a second experimentally plausible regime distinct from the dry-film imine branch."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Direct hydrolysis of alanine nitrile to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "prolonged heating in volcanic hot-spring or subaerial geothermal waters; acidic or mildly basic microenvironments accelerate hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "Successive hydration of the nitrile followed by cleavage yields the carboxylic acid and releases ammonia.", "reasoning": "Heated aqueous hydrolysis of alpha-aminonitriles gives amino acids with NH3 release. The product is represented as an alanine pool without fixed stereochemistry because achiral synthesis and hot aqueous processing do not reliably preserve a single enantiomer."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Partial hydrolysis of alanine nitrile to alaninamide", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "330-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "geothermal aqueous films or pools with moderate heating"}, "mechanism": "First hydration of the nitrile furnishes the corresponding amide.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydrolysis is mechanistically stepwise, and an amide is the chemically expected intermediate. Including alaninamide keeps oxygen accounting explicit and avoids the imbalances common in oversimplified prebiotic schemes."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrolysis of alaninamide to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "continued heating in the same geothermal waters that support amide formation"}, "mechanism": "Amide hydrolysis converts the carboxamide to the carboxylic acid and releases ammonia.", "reasoning": "Alaninamide is a balanceable and chemically standard intermediate on the route to alanine. Writing NH3 explicitly corrects a common omission in abbreviated nitrile-to-amino-acid schemes."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrolysis of lactonitrile to lactic acid", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "hot-spring or subaerial geothermal waters with prolonged contact times"}, "mechanism": "Hydration and cleavage of the nitrile group convert the cyanohydrin nitrile into the corresponding alpha-hydroxy acid.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin nitriles hydrolyze to alpha-hydroxy acids under heated aqueous conditions. This provides a chemically distinct branch from the same HCN-acetaldehyde pool without any pyruvate shortcut."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Mineral-mediated oxidation of lactic acid to pyruvic acid", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ni-Fe sulfide surfaces", "magnetite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370K", "pressure": "1 atm to mildly elevated in porous hot-spring conduits", "catalyst": "Ni-Fe sulfide surfaces / magnetite", "aqueous": true, "other": "subaerial geothermal field with metal-rich crusts; H2 escape helps pull dehydrogenation"}, "mechanism": "An alpha-hydroxy acid is dehydrogenated on redox-active mineral surfaces to the corresponding alpha-keto acid, with H2 as the explicit reduced product.", "reasoning": "Lactate/pyruvate is a plausible mineral-mediated redox couple under anoxic geothermal conditions. This is less experimentally secure than Strecker chemistry, but it is chemically defensible when H2 is written explicitly as the electron sink, avoiding any hidden oxidant."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Reductive amination of pyruvic acid to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ni-Fe sulfide surfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370K", "pressure": "1 atm to mildly elevated in porous hot-spring conduits", "catalyst": "Ni-Fe sulfide surfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "NH3-rich reducing geothermal pores with dissolved or freshly generated H2"}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate first forms an imine or iminium with ammonia, then H2-driven hydrogenation on metal sulfides gives alanine.", "reasoning": "Pyruvate can be converted to alanine only if both the nitrogen source and the reductant are explicit. Including H2 and H2O makes the redox chemistry transparent and repairs the reductant-free pyruvate-to-alanine shortcut that often fails stoichiometrically."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Chiral mineral-interface enrichment of L-alanine", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["chiral calcite or alpha-quartz step edges"], "conditions": {"temperature": "280-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "chiral mineral step edges", "aqueous": true, "other": "adsorption-desorption cycling at mineral interfaces in prebiotic pools"}, "mechanism": "Diastereomeric adsorption at chiral mineral step edges partitions alanine enantiomers, allowing recovery of an L-enriched fraction after repeated cycles.", "reasoning": "This is a physical resolution step, not a claim that a racemic pool quantitatively converts into one enantiomer. The target node denotes the isolated L-enriched fraction accumulated at or recovered from the mineral interface after repeated partitioning cycles."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Eutectic crystallization enrichment of L-alanine", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["evaporite salts"], "conditions": {"temperature": "280-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "repeated evaporation-dissolution cycling in saline prebiotic pools; eutectic or fractional crystallization regime"}, "mechanism": "Differential crystal growth, solubility, and phase partitioning during wet-dry cycles enrich the harvestable L-alanine fraction.", "reasoning": "Repeated crystallization can preserve and amplify slight stochastic or surface-induced enantiomeric imbalances in amino-acid pools. As in rxn_011, the target node represents the recovered L-enriched fraction rather than total conversion of the whole alanine pool."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "One-pot Strecker direct hydrolysis with mineral selection", "description": "Acetaldehyde, NH3, and HCN form alanine nitrile in one aqueous Strecker step; hot aqueous hydrolysis gives alanine, and chiral mineral interfaces isolate an L-enriched fraction.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "One-pot Strecker direct hydrolysis with eutectic selection", "description": "The same one-pot Strecker branch is followed by direct aminonitrile hydrolysis and then eutectic crystallization to recover L-enriched alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "One-pot Strecker amide branch with mineral selection", "description": "Alanine nitrile from the overall Strecker route first hydrolyzes to alaninamide, then to alanine, before chiral mineral partitioning yields L-alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_i