ptorless or coupled to a mineral redox cycle.", "Define the metal surface and H2 availability for rxn_010, or tie it to an experimentally supported prebiotic analogue.", "Relabel rxn_011 as mineral-interface enrichment rather than Biochemical."]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_003": 0.88, "rxn_008": 0.7, "rxn_009": 0.6, "rxn_010": 0.73, "rxn_012": 0.56}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.66, "pathway_coherence": 0.72, "environmental_progression": 0.64, "target_achievement": 0.71, "overall_confidence": 0.68, "feedback": "This is the least secure pathway because it combines the more speculative redox branch with the weaker eutectic selection step. rxn_008 needs a second H2O, rxn_009 and rxn_010 need tighter catalytic definition, and rxn_012 should be framed as conditional enrichment in an ice or brine environment rather than a generic Biochemical step.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_012"], "suggestions": ["Correct rxn_008 to mol_009 + 2 mol_005 -> mol_010 + mol_002.", "Provide explicit catalyst, temperature, and gas conditions for rxn_009 and rxn_010.", "Relabel rxn_012 as eutectic or ice-brine enrichment and clarify whether any initial alanine ee is assumed."]}], "best_pathway_id": "P3", "overall_confidence": 0.79, "network_feedback": "Connected 13-molecule and 12-reaction network with good internal consistency and no unbalanceable reactions. The deterministic balance check shows that rxn_005 and rxn_008 are salvageable but each omit one H2O as written; they should be corrected to the 2 H2O hydrolyses. The strongest chemistry is the Strecker core, especially the explicit amide-mediated hydrolysis routes P3 and P7. The cyanohydrin-lactate-pyruvate branch is less secure because rxn_009 and rxn_010 need much more specific catalytic and environmental support. The main target-level weakness is that rxn_011 and rxn_012 are enrichment or resolution operations rather than true chiral synthesis, and their environment is mislabeled as Biochemical instead of mineral-interface or ice-brine selection; they support at best an isolated L-enriched fraction from an alanine pool, not a robust deterministic origin of L-alanine from an exactly racemic pool. Overall the network is credible for generating an alanine pool but only moderately supported for the stated L-alanine target, so it does not meet the 0.9 approval threshold."}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 300813.129901886, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T08:46:31.350449", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: L-Alanine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.41\n- Feedback: Network is connected and has sufficient node count, and it spans several chemically recognizable entries into alanine. However, validity is limited by the RDKit ground truth: 7/12 reactions are UNBALANCED, and rxn_007 is only balanceable with implausibly large coefficients, indicating mis-specified stoichiometry. The most repairable issues are rxn_003 (should release H2O), rxn_004 (should release NH3 and needs 2 H2O overall), rxn_006 (should release NH3), rxn_007 (likely missing H2O), rxn_008 (needs NH3 and CO2 loss and/or an N-carbamoyl intermediate), and rxn_011 (needs an explicit reductant and H2O byproduct). The weakest chemistry is rxn_002, which gives no plausible oxygen sink or reductant for glycolaldehyde -> acetaldehyde, and rxn_010, which should not map lactonitrile directly to pyruvate without separate hydrolysis to lactate-type products and an oxidation step. rxn_012 is environmentally inconsistent as labeled ('Biochemical' vs mineral-mediated) and does not justify direct conversion of DL-alanine to pure L-alanine; at best it could represent a partial physical resolution. Broad alignment with prebiotic literature is strongest for the Strecker/cyanohydrin parts and weakest for rxn_002, rxn_010, and the chiral-selection step. Check mol_008 as well, because its InChI failed RDKit parsing. As written, the network robustly supports at most DL-alanine through some branches and is not approved for L-alanine.\n- Issues: ['Core Strecker chemistry from acetaldehyde is the strongest branch in the network. rxn_003 is chemically plausible but should expel H2O, and rxn_004 should release NH3 and use two waters overall. Surface-to-hydrothermal transfer is assumed rather than justified, and rxn_012 is better treated as mineral resolution than a biochemical reaction. This pathway supports DL-alanine much better than isolated L-alanine.', 'The aminonitrile -> amide -> amino acid sequence is coherent and rxn_005 is properly balanced, but rxn_003 still omits H2O and rxn_006 omits NH3. Keeping both hydrolyses on a generic surface is less convincing than heated aqueous processing. As with all branches, rxn_012 does not yet demonstrate production of pure L-alanine.', 'Hydantoin chemistry is a recognized amino acid entry, but this implementation is under-specified. rxn_007 only balances with implausible coefficients, suggesting a missing H2O product, and rxn_008 needs additional byproducts such as CO2 and NH3 or an explicit N-carbamoyl alanine intermediate. The final enantioenrichment step remains the limiting issue.', 'Cyanohydrin formation is strong, but the route breaks at pyruvate generation. rxn_010 should not convert lactonitrile directly to pyruvate by simple hydrolysis; a lactate-type intermediate and a separate oxidation step are needed. rxn_011 also lacks the reductant required for reductive amination. The branch does not convincingly reach L-alanine as written.', 'This is only a terminal segment rather than a full feedstock-to-target route, and it relies on an under-specified pyruvate chemistry. rxn_011 needs an explicit reducing system and proper stoichiometry, because NH3 alone will not reductively aminate pyruvate. Combined with the weak chiral-selection step, target achievement is low.', 'The formaldehyde -> glycolaldehyde entry is plausible, but rxn_002 is the weakest single reaction in the network: glycolaldehyde -> acetaldehyde is unbalanced and lacks a credible redox partner or oxygen sink. Downstream Strecker chemistry is reasonable after stoichiometric repair. The pathway still reaches at best DL-alanine robustly, not well-supported L-alanine.', 'This branch combines a plausible formose-type start with a weaker midstream deoxygenation. rxn_002 remains a major bottleneck, and rxn_003/rxn_006 both omit expected byproducts. The amide branch is coherent once alanine nitrile exists, but the overall path is still undermined by the unsupported acetaldehyde step and the unresolved final L-enrichment.', 'The hydantoin branch inherits the same weak glycolaldehyde -> acetaldehyde step and adds further stoichiometric problems in the hydantoin chemistry. rxn_007 appears to be missing H2O, and rxn_008 needs NH3/CO2 loss or an explicit carbamoyl intermediate. The path is chemically recognizable but not valid enough as written.', 'This is the least coherent full upstream route. It depends on the unconvincing deoxygenation rxn_002, the flawed lactonitrile -> pyruvate shortcut in rxn_010, the under-specified reductive amination rxn_011, and the weak final enantioenrichment step. Too many key nodes are chemically or stoichiometrically unsupported.', 'Starting from glycolaldehyde shortens the upstream branch, but the deoxygenation to acetaldehyde is still a major unresolved step. Once acetaldehyde is available, the Strecker/hydrolysis chemistry is reasonable after stoichiometric correction. The path again supports DL-alanine far better than a validated route to L-alanine.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.79\n- Feedback: Connected 13-molecule and 12-reaction network with good internal consistency and no unbalanceable reactions. The deterministic balance check shows that rxn_005 and rxn_008 are salvageable but each omit one H2O as written; they should be corrected to the 2 H2O hydrolyses. The strongest chemistry is the Strecker core, especially the explicit amide-mediated hydrolysis routes P3 and P7. The cyanohydrin-lactate-pyruvate branch is less secure because rxn_009 and rxn_010 need much more specific catalytic and environmental support. The main target-level weakness is that rxn_011 and rxn_012 are enrichment or resolution operations rather than true chiral synthesis, and their environment is mislabeled as Biochemical instead of mineral-interface or ice-brine selection; they support at best an isolated L-enriched fraction from an alanine pool, not a robust deterministic origin of L-alanine from an exactly racemic pool. Overall the network is credible for generating an alanine pool but only moderately supported for the stated L-alanine target, so it does not meet the 0.9 approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['Short and coherent Strecker route to an alanine pool. The main issues are rxn_005, which is one H2O short as written and is mechanistically better viewed through alaninamide, and rxn_011, which is an enrichment or separation step with contingent L-selectivity rather than a robust chiral synthesis.', 'This is still a concise Strecker route, but the direct hydrolysis issue in rxn_005 remains, and rxn_012 is a weaker final step because eutectic enrichment from a nominally racemic alanine pool is not strongly established as a deterministic source of an L-enriched fraction.', 'The core chemistry to alanine is good, and the amide branch is more convincing than direct nitrile hydrolysis. The limiting factor is rxn_012: eutectic selection is better framed as conditional enrichment or partitioning than as a robust route from an exactly racemic pool to a defined L product.', 'The dry-film imine route is chemically plausible, but it implicitly needs a wet-dry cycle: dehydration for rxn_001, cyanide addition for rxn_002, then rehydration for hydrolysis. rxn_005 is again one H2O short as written, and the final L-enrichment remains conditional.', 'This route has the same wet-dry handling issue as P5, plus the weaker eutectic final step. The chemistry to an alanine pool is reasonable, but rxn_005 should be corrected and rxn_012 needs a more explicit and better justified ice or brine setting.', 'The imine plus amide route is chemically sound up to alanine, but the required dry-to-wet transition is only implicit and the eutectic selection step is still the main weakness for the stated L target.', 'This branch is connected and redox-explicit, but it is the most speculative route. rxn_008 is one H2O short as written, rxn_009 needs much firmer catalytic and environmental support for lactate-to-pyruvate dehydrogenation with H2 release, and rxn_010 needs explicit metal and H2 conditions.', 'This is the least secure pathway because it combines the more speculative redox branch with the weaker eutectic selection step. rxn_008 needs a second H2O, rxn_009 and rxn_010 need tighter catalytic definition, and rxn_012 should be framed as conditional enrichment in an ice or brine environment rather than a generic Biochemical step.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-alanine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Use geochemically plausible C2 feedstocks (acetylene and ethanol) to converge on acetaldehyde in geothermal surface settings. From acetaldehyde, exploit three chemically supported alanine-precursor branches: Strecker aminonitrile formation followed by stepwise hydrolysis, Strecker aminonitrile formation followed by direct net hydrolysis, and Bucherer-Bergs-type hydantoin formation followed by hydrolysis. Because achiral prebiotic synthesis is expected to furnish a racemic alanine pool, L-alanine is obtained only in the final stage by explicit mineral-surface resolution, with calcite- and quartz-mediated partitioning treated as alternative conditional routes.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H6O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ethanol\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alanine nitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alaninamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-methylimidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"rac-5-methylhydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic hydration of acetylene to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"acidic sulfur-rich hot-spring water; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proton-assisted addition of water to acetylene gives vinyl alcohol, which tautomerizes to acetaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acetylene is a geochemically plausible C2 feedstock in volcanic or impact-processed surface settings. Acidic hot-spring waters can hydrate a C\u2261C bond and the enol product tautomerizes cleanly to acetaldehyde, avoiding the oxygen-sink problem that affects unsupported glycolaldehyde-to-acetaldehyde shortcuts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Magnetite-mediated ethanol dehydrogenation to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"steam-rich or transiently dry geothermal mineral surface; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-mediated dehydrogenation of the primary alcohol yields acetaldehyde and molecular hydrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"If prebiotic ethanol is available, its conversion to acetaldehyde is redox-explicit because H2 carries away the excess hydrogen. Magnetite-rich geothermal surfaces make this a more defensible acetaldehyde source than any unbalanced deoxygenation step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of alanine nitrile from acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN/NH3-rich wet-dry cycles in evaporitic pools\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde condenses with ammonia to an imine or iminium species, and cyanide adds to furnish the aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the strongest core branch in the network. Strecker chemistry from acetaldehyde requires no molecular oxygen, gives the correct C3/N2 framework directly, and includes water as the necessary byproduct.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"heated hot-spring or evaporitic wet phase\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic water addition to the nitrile followed by tautomerization furnishes the amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitriles hydrolyze stepwise, and the amide is the expected first oxygenated intermediate. Representing the amide explicitly avoids compressing two hydrolyses into one under overly mild conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of alaninamide to racemic alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"prolonged heating in anoxic water\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amide hydrolysis via a tetrahedral intermediate yields the carboxylic acid and liberates ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The second hydrolysis step releases ammonia explicitly and is a chemically conservative route from aminonitrile chemistry to a racemic alanine pool.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct hot-water hydrolysis of alanine nitrile to racemic alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"extended residence in hot-spring water\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis are represented as a single net transformation under harsher aqueous heating.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This shortcut is less gentle than the explicit amide route but is stoichiometrically correct: the net transformation requires two waters and releases ammonia. It represents longer or hotter aqueous processing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type formation of 5-methylhydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"carbonate-rich wet-dry films in evaporitic pools\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde undergoes cyanide/ammonia addition chemistry, captures CO2 as a carbamyl equivalent, and cyclizes with dehydration to hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin chemistry is a legitimate alternative branch from the same acetaldehyde feedstock. Carbonate availability makes Bucherer-Bergs-type trapping attractive in evaporitic pools, producing a more stable amino-acid precursor reservoir.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic opening of 5-methylhydantoin to racemic alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"prolonged hydrolysis in hot-spring water\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ring opening yields carbamylated alanine-like intermediates that hydrolyze and decarboxylate to alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis is written with the required CO2 and NH3 outputs, fixing the under-specified chemistry common in simplified schemes. It supplies alanine from a more protected precursor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Calcite-surface resolution of racemic alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"calcite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"calcite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"carbonate-rich shoreline; repeated dissolution and reprecipitation on calcite step and kink sites\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Differential adsorption and crystallization on carbonate step sites partitions the racemate into separable L-rich and D-rich phases.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a resolution step rather than a chiral synthesis step. Calcite surfaces can impose local enantioselective partitioning for amino acids; repeated wet-dry cycling can therefore yield complementary L- and D-enriched fractions. The network represents the isolated L-rich fraction as the target.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Quartz-surface resolution of racemic alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"alpha-quartz\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"alpha-quartz\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycling on locally single-handed quartz grains\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Adsorption and desorption on crystallographically chiral quartz faces partition the two enantiomers into separable phases.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This alternative route uses locally single-handed quartz faces rather than carbonate surfaces. The global availability of a net handed surface is uncertain, so the step is explicitly conditional, but it offers a second chemically plausible route for isolating L-enriched alanine from a racemic pool.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene-Strecker-amide-calcite route\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetylene is hydrated to acetaldehyde, converted by Strecker chemistry to alanine nitrile, hydrolyzed through the amide, and resolved on calcite to isolate L-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene-Strecker-amide-quartz route\",\n      \"description\": \"The same acetylene-to-Strecker-amide sequence is followed by quartz-surface resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene-Strecker-direct-hydrolysis-calcite route\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetylene-derived acetaldehyde enters Strecker chemistry, and alanine nitrile is hydrolyzed directly under hotter aqueous conditions before calcite resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene-Strecker-direct-hydrolysis-quartz route\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetylene hydration and Strecker chemistry are followed by net nitrile hydrolysis and quartz-based enantiomeric partitioning.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene-Bucherer-Bergs-calcite route\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetylene supplies acetaldehyde, which is trapped as hydantoin in carbonate-rich cyanide/ammonia chemistry and then hydrolyzed to alanine before calcite resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Ethanol-Strecker-amide-calcite route\",\n      \"description\": \"Ethanol is dehydrogenated to acetaldehyde on magnetite, then the standard Strecker-amide branch yields DL-alanine for calcite-mediated resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Ethanol-Strecker-amide-quartz route\",\n      \"description\": \"The ethanol entry merges into the Strecker-amide branch and reaches L-alanine through quartz-surface resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Ethanol-Strecker-direct-hydrolysis-calcite route\",\n      \"description\": \"After ethanol dehydrogenation to acetaldehyde, Strecker chemistry and direct hot-water nitrile hydrolysis produce DL-alanine for calcite resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Ethanol-Bucherer-Bergs-calcite route\",\n      \"description\": \"Ethanol-derived acetaldehyde is converted through the hydantoin branch, then hydrolyzed and resolved on calcite.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Ethanol-Bucherer-Bergs-quartz route\",\n      \"description\": \"The ethanol entry feeds Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin formation and hydrolysis, followed by quartz-mediated enantiomeric partitioning to isolate L-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [